A||||4||Rod101||Field Service mode code
Z||000000||Rod101||08-05-99||10:41 PM||||When I short the diagnose mode (the A-B short thing) when the car run, it is in field service mode right? What does flashing every one second mean? I am on the freeway.<p>
Z||000001||theogre||08-05-99||10:49 PM||||NEVER jump A-B when driving. Shorting those terminals should only be done for exactly 2 reasons.<p>1. reading ECM error codes.<p>2. setting curb timing on distributor motors.<p>Only an ECM SCANNER, not a code reader, can be used to read the ECM in real time.
Z||000002||Rod101||08-05-99||11:17 PM||||I was reading Chilton repair manual, pg 4-21. A little quote :<p> "When the test terminal is grounded with the engine running, it will cause the ECM to enter Field Service Mode. In this mode, the service engine soon light will indicate whether the system is in Open or Close Loop opearation. In OL, the light will flash 2.5 times per second, in CL, the light will stay out most of the time if the system is too lean and will stay on most of the time if the system is too rich."<p>Well. Mine is flashing on-off every second interval.<p>Either case, have I fired my ECM? PANIC?<p>
Z||000003||ChadMan||08-06-99||07:07 AM||||I'm guessing it's working just fine, Rod.  My mechanic buddy hooked my car up to his scanner. I was concerned when it kept flicking back and forth from slightly lean to slightly rich, but he told me that's normal - the ECM will continually hunt for an ideal mixture and your car will always vacillate between slightly lean/slightly rich.  Your car is probably in CL going from slightly lean (light off) to slightly rich (light on) and back.
Z||000004||theogre||08-06-99||07:59 AM||||yup, it'll do that.<p>Unless the ECM has a code set. It will go closed loop as soon as everything is warm, and stay there.<p>You likely didn't damage the ECM, but it's not a good idea. If you want meaningfull ECM real time data, get an ECM scanner. They aren't hugly expensive anymore. I got a nice one for about 150$us.
